@Yulia Popova - Here is a 'quick and dirty' riff on what the the settings could look like based on Anna Y's feedback on Slack, to move the control (checkbox or button) to the first position instead of at the end of the line: https://mozilla.slack.com/archives/CNP7Q51KN/p1710945711362139?thread_ts=1710870114.140289&cid=CNP7Q51KN

Also, I wanted to see whether we even need the categorical headers (like "Firefox Tools", "AI tools", etc.). For the MVP release of this, I'm wondering if we actually don't. 

In that way we can also have the header be descriptive - help the user know what action to take (Select items to pin to the sidebar)

@Katie Lower good point about notifications. I think it does make sense to include it here as well.
Regarding extensions: I would assume we show sidebar-specific extensions that have been installed. Wondering if we should feature some other extensions?:thinking_face:
Regarding the "show more" pattern - good point. I can look into it. My initial thought was that we would have a scroll bar.

@Ania Safko and @Eric Pang - Is there any difference, conceptually, between a "Tab folder" and a "Tab group"? Or, is it the same concept by different names?

When I look at the visuals in this section, tab folders seem to be the same thing as tab groups (or, rather, how tab groups have been implemented in other browsers). 

Let me know if I'm missing any nuance or context here -

@Yulia Popova might be a crazy idea, but I'm wondering if the same idea:
* hover states on destinations will be actionable (e.g., show you your recently closed tabs), 
could be applied on action buttons? Could + by default create a new tab, but also give users an option to start "a new" on hover - to save all the currently open tabs into a neat little "session" stack that doesn't interfere with their new session and allows for a mental blank slate?

@Yulia Popova @Eric Pang @Emanuela Damiani a part of me wonders if it makes sense to apply the idea of frecency to tab management in the sidebar? 
For "inactive" (non-foregrounded tabs): Expose most frequent action - close the tab (e.g., on-hover) for all but the foreground tab and let the right click menu contain the less frequent ones (copy link, move, etc).

For "active" (foreground tab), expose more actions right away since users are more likely to engage with them (e.g., close, mute for tabs with audio stream, make a screenshot, share)?
